`seen` maps value → index. At each `x` you ask whether `target - x` was already walked past; if so, that earlier index and `i` are the answer. The `if` sits above `seen[x] = i`, so the current element is never in the map when you check it — that's what stops an element from pairing with itself (the `[3,3], target=6` case still works because the first `3` is stored before the second one is checked).

